    "result_text": "The full suite passes (22 tests, 0 failures/errors) and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the count of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, mirroring how the existing `customerCode` computed field works.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function class) \u2014 reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, counts existing owners from `ownerRepository.findAll()` whose firstName and lastName match (lower-cased comparison), and stores the count on the owner. Runs before `save`, so the new owner is not counted.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name onto the GET response.\n\n5. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column to the `owners` table. Tests run on hsqldb.\n\nThe Cp13 acceptance test (two owners with the same first+last name, second expecting `namesakeCount = 1`) passes, and all 22 tests remain green.",
